---
import Shot from "../../../components/Shot.astro";
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
---

<DocsLayout
  docKey="concepts"
  title="Stacks, renders and the Library"
  description="What Deptheos means by a stack, a render and the Library, how past renders relate to your settings, and what happens to your files."
>
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>Stacks, renders and the Library</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Deptheos keeps every result you make, with the frames and settings that made it, so you can try another
      method, compare the two and go back to either. These are the words the app uses for the pieces.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section id="bracket">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>A focus bracket, and a stack</h2>
      <p>
        A <em>focus bracket</em> is the set of photos you shoot of one subject, each focused a little nearer or
        farther than the one before, so that every part of the subject is sharp in at least one of them. Those
        photos are the <em>frames</em>. Stacking lines the frames up and combines the sharp part of each into
        one picture.
      </p>
      <p>
        In the app, a <em>stack</em> is one subject's frames and everything made from them. It is named after
        the frames' folder until you rename it (Edit ▸ Rename Stack…). A stack is one tile in the Library, and
        the Stacking screen shows one stack at a time.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="render">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>A render</h2>
      <p>
        Each time you press Stack, the result is saved as a <em>render</em>, numbered within its stack: #1, #2
        and so on. A render keeps the picture, its list of frames with which were included and which excluded,
        the method and its settings, and any retouching painted on it. Renders are listed in the
        <em>Renders</em> strip along the bottom of the Stacking screen. Stack again with another method and you
        get #2 beside #1, ready to <a href="/docs/compare/">compare</a>. Rename Render… gives one a name of its
        own after its number ("#4 · sharper"), and Star Render (<em>K</em>) marks the one you want to keep.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="working">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Past renders and your current settings</h2>
      <p>
        Renders are read-only snapshots. The Frames list and the inspector always hold your current setup for
        the next Stack, and you can edit them at any time. Once you change a frame or setting after stacking,
        the line under the method counts the changes ("1 change to the settings since #4") with a
        <em>Revert</em> button to restore #4's frames and settings (Stack ▸ Revert to #4 does the same). The
        title bar names the render on screen ("#4 · Pyramid · 46 frames") with an <em>Edited</em> label under
        it, and #4's tile in the strip is marked Edited too.
      </p>
      <p>
        Click any past render in the strip to view its result. Your Frames list and settings stay untouched, so
        clicking through earlier renders never loses unstacked tweaks, and pressing Stack still uses what is in
        the inspector. The line under the method shows how your current settings differ from the render on
        screen ("1 difference from #3 in the settings", or "Same frames and settings as #3"), and tick marks on
        the sliders show #3's values.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <em>Revert</em> beside that line (also Stack ▸ Revert to #3, or Revert to #3 in a render's right-click
          menu) loads #3's frames and settings back into the list and inspector so you can tweak and stack from
          there. If you have unstacked changes, Deptheos asks before replacing them; your saved renders are
          always kept either way.
        </li>
        <li>
          <em>Render Info</em> (<em>⌘I</em>, Stack ▸ Render Info, or a render's right-click menu) lists what made
          the render: its method and sliders, alignment, RAW decoder and frames, with a dot beside any value
          that differs from your current setup.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="library">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>The Library</h2>
      <p>
        The Library is the home screen: every stack as a tile, newest day first, with the stacks a batch made in
        a section of their own. A tile shows the stack's starred render, or else its newest, and how many renders
        it has. The bar on the left shows <em>All stacks</em>, <em>Starred</em> or <em>Failed</em> and jumps to
        Today, Yesterday, Previous 7 days or Earlier; the search field matches stack and render names and batch names.
        Select a tile to list its renders in the inspector on the right, and double-click it (or press ↩) to open it in
        Stacking. A render in the inspector works the same way: a click selects it, and a double-click or ↩
        opens its stack showing that render. ⌥⌘2 and ⌥⌘1 switch between the Library and Stacking.
      </p>
      <p>
        Right-click a tile to rename, star, export or delete. A delete moves the stack or render to
        <em>Recently Deleted</em>, where it is kept for 30 days and can be restored; the notice after it has
        Undo. <em>Keep Only Starred</em> deletes a stack's renders that aren't starred.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="shot"><Shot name="library" width="2880" height="1800" loading="lazy" alt="The Library showing stacked hornet and circuit board stacks, the left filter sidebar, and the selected stack's renders in the inspector." /><figcaption>The Library: stacks by day, renders in the inspector.</figcaption></figure>
  </section>

  <section id="files">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Where renders are kept, and your originals</h2>
      <p>
        Your frames are only ever read. Deptheos never changes, moves or deletes them, and deleting a render or
        a stack leaves them alone. Renders are kept in the app's own folder, not beside your photos:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>macOS: <code>~/Library/Application Support/Deptheos</code></li>
        <li>Windows: <code>%LOCALAPPDATA%\Deptheos</code></li>
        <li>Linux: <code>~/.local/share/Deptheos</code></li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Renders take room: each holds a full-size result. To keep them on another disk, choose a folder under
        Settings ▸ Library ▸ <em>Library folder</em>; it takes effect the next time Deptheos starts.
        <em>Delete Library…</em> in the same place deletes every stack and render, leaving your frames and
        exported files alone. <a href="/docs/library/">Your Library and backups</a> says what the Library holds
        and how to back it up.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="locate">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Moved frames and Locate</h2>
      <p>
        A render remembers where its frames were. If you move or rename their folder, the list marks those
        frames <em>Missing</em>, and a note over it offers <em>Locate…</em> and <em>Remove</em>. Locate… asks
        where the folder went, and points the list and every render that uses it at the new place. Stacking,
        retouching from a frame and comparing with a frame need the frames; looking at a render's result does
        not.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
